    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2054320721665392949",
      "url": "https://x.com/theydied4us/status/2054320721665392949",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/theydied4us/status/2054320721665392949",
      "text": "Hero of the Month - LCDR Radcliffe “Pete” Denniston Jr., USN\n\nLieutenant Commander Radcliffe “Pete” Denniston Jr. was born on February 5, 1916, in Wauwatosa, Wisconsin. A graduate of Lawrence College, he entered the Navy as an aviation cadet in 1936 and trained at Pensacola, Florida before joining Torpedo Squadron Five (VT-5) aboard the USS Yorktown (CV-5).\n\nWhen the Pacific front of WWII opened, Denniston participated in strikes against the Gilbert and Marshall Islands, Salamaua, Lae, and Tulagi. His most distinguished actions came during the Battle of the Coral Sea in May 1942, where he successfully scored torpedo hits on two Japanese carriers while facing intense anti-aircraft fire and enemy fighter opposition. For his extraordinary heroism during the attacks at Tulagi Harbor and Coral Sea, he was awarded the Navy Cross.\n\nFollowing the battle of Coral Sea, Denniston returned to Fort Lauderdale, Florida as an instructor and later became chief flight instructor. On September 25, 1943, he assumed command of Torpedo Squadron Eleven (VT-11).\n\nDuring his second combat tour, he took part in missions against Okinawa, Formosa, Luzon, Leyte, and the Second Battle of the Philippine Sea. On November 13, 1944, while leading a glide-bombing attack against enemy shipping in Manila Harbor, his aircraft encountered heavy anti-aircraft fire. Denniston’s final transmission was: “Plane hit, am going in.” He was never seen again.\n\nLCDR Denniston is buried at the ABMC Manila American Cemetery in the Philippines.\n\nYour donation to ABMF helps preserve the memories of those who fought and died so we can live as free people. https://t.co/xsExHblXKi",
